closest_species function find closest species Fish Bioenergetics 4.0 database: can get information included dataset, fb4_data, get source species: can use bem_from_fb4 function get bioenergetics parameters species, supply closest species name. also include parameters Fish Bioenergetics 4.0 software, proportion maximum consumption parameters. values Troia Perkin (2022) Micropterus treculii probably wrong Thymallus arcticus. might want see species grow various places Tennesssee, need information water temperature places. One source US Geological Survey (USGS) databases, information. let’s find sites Tennesssee water temperature data, using wrapper dataRetrieval package: First 5 stations Tennessee water temperature data Let’s see ’d Little River: , need water temperature site. Let’s get 2023, padding bit side can get full year data: measured manually, lot data points. Let’s plot data see looks like:  need points every day, let’s interpolate data. default, give us error lot missing data: default, 80% missing. can manually change threshold minimum_fraction_missing argument. Let’s set 0.5, give us error 50% data missing. can also focus just 2023 setting start_date end_date arguments: can see original plot:  ’s smoothed plot: keep actual temperatures fish experienced, just linearly interpolate . Now can compute weight gain fish site: can plot weight gain year:  can look every species database see done Little River TN. , make , bad assumptions: start weight 6.382417 g, joules per gram wet mass species’ prey, oxycalorific coefficient. knew pass vector one value per organism, including different values different life stages present. also assume happy living freshwater temperate mountain stream – unlikely species like lionfish, throw .  can also see organism survive (go zero body mass) Little River TN given (bad) assumptions going analysis: organisms survive Alewife (adult), Atlantic cod (juvenile & adult) , Atlantic menhaden (YOY), Baltic herring (YOY), Bay anchovy (juvenile & adult), Bluegill sunfish (adult), Bluegill sunfish (juvenile), Brook Trout (juvenile & adult), Brown Trout, Burbot (juvenile & adult), California Killifish (juvenile & adult), Chinook salmon (adult), Coho salmon (adult), Cutthroat trout, Dace (adult & juvenile), Eurasian perch (1 g), Eurasian perch (100 g), Eurasian perch (larvae & YOY), European anchovy (adult), European anchovy (egg & larvae), European anchovy (juvenile), Fathead minnow, Gizzard shad, Herring (adult), Herring (juvenile), Humpback chub (juvenile & sub-adult), Indo-Pacific Lionfish (juvenile & adult), Lake trout (adult), Lingcod , Nile perch, North Sea cod, Northern pikeminnow, Pallid sturgeon (juvenile), Pallid sturgeon (larvae), Pink salmon (adult), Plains killifish , Rainbow Trout (adult) , Rainbow Trout (juvenile) , Red River shiner, Ruffe, Sacramento perch, Saugeye, Sea lamprey, Smallmouth bass (adult), Smallmouth Bass (T<=26 & sub-adult & adult), Smallmouth Bass (T>26 & sub-adult & adult), Snakehead (juvenile), Sockeye salmon (adult), Steelhead (adult), Striped bass (larvae), Tilapia (adult), Walleye (adult), Walleye (larvae & juvenile), Walleye pollock (>400 g), Walleye pollock (100-299 g), Walleye pollock (300-400 g), Walleye pollock (adult), Walleye pollock (juvenile), Western mosquitofish (juvenile & adult), White bass (larvae), Yellow perch (adult), Yellow perch (juvenile), Yellow perch (larvae), Zander (adult), Zebra mussel (adult). bad analysis, shows use fishyenergy package get bioenergetics parameters species, find water temperature data, compute weight gain species site – use better data!","code":"library(fishyenergy) closest_species <- fb4_closest_species(\"Thymallus arcticus\") #> [1] \"Sources for Thymallus arcticus baicalensis are: Hartman and Jensen 2016\" closest_species #> [1] \"Thymallus arcticus baicalensis\" print(fb4_data$Source[fb4_data$Sci_Name == closest_species]) #> [1] \"Hartman and Jensen 2016\" BEM <- bem_from_fb4(closest_species, CP=0.5, FA=0.1018428, UA=0.08743042, SDA=0.150819, ED=3983.646) stations <- water_stations_find(state=\"TN\") knitr::kable(head(stations[,1:6], 5), caption=\"First 5 stations in Tennessee with water temperature data\") little_river_row <- stations[stations$station_nm == \"LITTLE RIVER ABOVE TOWNSEND, TN\",] little_river_id <- paste0(\"USGS-\", little_river_row$site_no) raw_temps <- daily_temperature_raw(little_river_id, start_date=\"2022-11-01\", end_date=\"2024-02-01\") plot(raw_temps$ActivityStartDate, raw_temps$ResultMeasureValue, pch=19, xlab=\"Date\", ylab=\"Temperature (C)\", main=\"Little River Temperature Data\", bty=\"n\") interpolated_temps <- daily_temperature_interpolate(raw_temps, minimum_fraction_missing=0.05, start_date=\"2023-01-01\", end_date=\"2023-12-31\") plot(raw_temps$ActivityStartDate, raw_temps$ResultMeasureValue, pch=19, xlab=\"Date\", ylab=\"Temperature (C)\", main=\"Little River Temperature Data\", bty=\"n\") points(interpolated_temps$date, interpolated_temps$temp, col=\"red\", pch='.') station_result <- single_station_compute(T_vector=interpolated_temps$temp, BEM=BEM) plot(interpolated_temps$date, station_result$W2_cum, xlab=\"Date\", ylab=\"Mass (g)\", type='l', bty=\"n\", main=\"Weight Gain in Little River\") all_organisms <- loop_species_across_station(interpolated_temps$temp)  all_organisms$SpeciesStage <- all_organisms$common  for (i in 1:nrow(all_organisms)) {   if (nchar(all_organisms$life_stage[i]) > 0) {     all_organisms$common[i] <- paste0(all_organisms$SpeciesStage[i], \" (\", all_organisms$life_stage[i], \")\")   } }  somewhat_surviving_organisms <- all_organisms[!is.na(all_organisms$W2_cum),]     weird_taxa <- unique(c(subset(somewhat_surviving_organisms, ProportionStartingWeight < 0.5 | ProportionStartingWeight > 25)$SpeciesStage, all_organisms$SpeciesStage[is.na(all_organisms$W2_cum)]))   feasible_organisms <- somewhat_surviving_organisms[!somewhat_surviving_organisms$SpeciesStage %in% weird_taxa,]  library(ggplot2) library(ggrepel) library(dplyr)  last_points <- feasible_organisms |>   group_by(SpeciesStage) |>   slice_max(order_by = julian, n = 1)   ggplot(feasible_organisms, aes(x=julian, y=ProportionStartingWeight, color=SpeciesStage)) +   geom_line() + theme_minimal() +   geom_text_repel(     data = last_points,     aes(label = SpeciesStage),     hjust = 0, nudge_x = 5, size = 3, show.legend = FALSE, segment.color = 'gray', box.padding = 0.3, max.overlaps = Inf   )  +   labs(x=\"Day of year\", y=\"Relative mass\") + xlim(c(0, 500)) + theme(legend.position = \"none\") dead_organisms <- sort(unique(somewhat_surviving_organisms$SpeciesStage[which(somewhat_surviving_organisms$W2_cum<0)]))"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/authors.html","id":null,"dir":"","previous_headings":"","what":"Authors","title":"Authors and Citation","text":"Brian O'Meara. Author, maintainer.","code":""},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/authors.html","id":"citation","dir":"","previous_headings":"","what":"Citation","title":"Authors and Citation","text":"Troia, J M, Perkin, S J (2022). “Can fisheries bioenergetics modelling refine spatially explicit assessments climate change vulnerability?” Conservation Physiology, 10(1), coac035. ISSN 2051-1434, doi:10.1093/conphys/coac035, https://academic.oup.com/conphys/article/doi/10.1093/conphys/coac035/6619479. Deslauriers, David, Chipps, R. S, Breck, E. J, Rice, . J, Madenjian, P. C (2017). “Fish 'Bioenergetics' 4.0: R-Based Modeling Application.” Fisheries, 42(11), 586–596. ISSN 0363-2415, 1548-8446, doi:10.1080/03632415.2017.1377558, https://academic.oup.com/fisheries/article/42/11/586/7833943.","code":"@Article{,   title = {Can fisheries bioenergetics modelling refine spatially explicit assessments of climate change vulnerability?},   volume = {10},   copyright = {https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/},   issn = {2051-1434},   url = {https://academic.oup.com/conphys/article/doi/10.1093/conphys/coac035/6619479},   doi = {10.1093/conphys/coac035},   language = {en},   number = {1},   urldate = {2025-06-03},   journal = {Conservation Physiology},   author = {{Troia} and Matthew J and {Perkin} and Joshuah S},   editor = {{Cooke} and {Steven}},   month = {jan},   year = {2022},   pages = {coac035}, } @Article{,   title = {Fish 'Bioenergetics' 4.0: An R-Based Modeling Application},   volume = {42},   copyright = {https://academic.oup.com/pages/standard-publication-reuse-rights},   issn = {0363-2415, 1548-8446},   shorttitle = {Fish 'Bioenergetics' 4.0},   url = {https://academic.oup.com/fisheries/article/42/11/586/7833943},   doi = {10.1080/03632415.2017.1377558},   language = {en},   number = {11},   urldate = {2025-06-03},   journal = {Fisheries},   author = {{Deslauriers} and {David} and {Chipps} and Steven R. and {Breck} and James E. and {Rice} and James A. and {Madenjian} and Charles P.},   month = {nov},   year = {2017},   pages = {586--596}, }"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/index.html","id":"fishyenergy","dir":"","previous_headings":"","what":"Predict Fish Mass Gain","title":"Predict Fish Mass Gain","text":"uses functions Troia Perkin (2022) predict gain fish mass using model originally Hanson et al. (1997). Funded NSF IOS-2222476 See Getting Started vignette quick introduction package External Data vignette get data Fish Bioenergetics 4.0 software USGS water temperature data.","code":""},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/reference/BEM_from_FB4.html","id":null,"dir":"Reference","previous_headings":"","what":"Create a BEM object from FB4 data — bem_from_fb4","title":"Create a BEM object from FB4 data — bem_from_fb4","text":"Create BEM object FB4 data","code":""},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/reference/BEM_from_FB4.html","id":"ref-usage","dir":"Reference","previous_headings":"","what":"Usage","title":"Create a BEM object from FB4 data — bem_from_fb4","text":"","code":"bem_from_fb4(   species,   lifestage = NULL,   CP = 0.5,   FA = 0.1018428,   UA = 0.08743042,   SDA = 0.150819