closest_species function find closest species Fish Bioenergetics 4.0 database: can get information included dataset, fb4_data, get source species: can use bem_from_fb4 function get bioenergetics parameters species, supply closest species name. also include parameters Fish Bioenergetics 4.0 software, proportion maximum consumption parameters. values Troia Perkin (2022) Micropterus treculii probably wrong Thymallus arcticus. might want see species grow various places Tennesssee, need information water temperature places. One source US Geological Survey (USGS) databases, information. let’s find sites Tennesssee water temperature data, using wrapper dataRetrieval package: First 5 stations Tennessee water temperature data Let’s see ’d Little River: , need water temperature site. Let’s get 2023, padding bit side can get full year data: measured manually, lot data points. Let’s plot data see looks like:  need points every day, let’s interpolate data. default, give us error lot missing data: default, 80% missing. can manually change threshold minimum_fraction_missing argument. Let’s set 0.5, give us error 50% data missing. can also focus just 2023 setting start_date end_date arguments: can see original plot:  ’s smoothed plot: keep actual temperatures fish experienced, just linearly interpolate . Now can compute weight gain fish site: can plot weight gain year:  can look every species database see done Little River TN. , make , bad assumptions: start weight 6.382417 g, joules per gram wet mass species’ prey, oxycalorific coefficient. knew pass vector one value per organism, including different values different life stages present. also assume happy living freshwater temperate mountain stream – unlikely species like lionfish, throw .  can also see organism survive (go zero body mass) Little River TN given (bad) assumptions going analysis: organisms survive Alewife (adult), Atlantic cod (juvenile & adult) , Atlantic menhaden (YOY), Baltic herring (YOY), Bay anchovy (juvenile & adult), Bluegill sunfish (adult), Bluegill sunfish (juvenile), Brook Trout (juvenile & adult), Brown Trout, Burbot (juvenile & adult), California Killifish (juvenile & adult), Chinook salmon (adult), Coho salmon (adult), Cutthroat trout, Dace (adult & juvenile), Eurasian perch (1 g), Eurasian perch (100 g), Eurasian perch (larvae & YOY), European anchovy (adult), European anchovy (egg & larvae), European anchovy (juvenile), Fathead minnow, Gizzard shad, Herring (adult), Herring (juvenile), Humpback chub (juvenile & sub-adult), Indo-Pacific Lionfish (juvenile & adult), Lake trout (adult), Lingcod , Nile perch, North Sea cod, Northern pikeminnow, Pallid sturgeon (juvenile), Pallid sturgeon (larvae), Pink salmon (adult), Plains killifish , Rainbow Trout (adult) , Rainbow Trout (juvenile) , Red River shiner, Ruffe, Sacramento perch, Saugeye, Sea lamprey, Smallmouth bass (adult), Smallmouth Bass (T<=26 & sub-adult & adult), Smallmouth Bass (T>26 & sub-adult & adult), Snakehead (juvenile), Sockeye salmon (adult), Steelhead (adult), Striped bass (larvae), Tilapia (adult), Walleye (adult), Walleye (larvae & juvenile), Walleye pollock (>400 g), Walleye pollock (100-299 g), Walleye pollock (300-400 g), Walleye pollock (adult), Walleye pollock (juvenile), Western mosquitofish (juvenile & adult), White bass (larvae), Yellow perch (adult), Yellow perch (juvenile), Yellow perch (larvae), Zander (adult), Zebra mussel (adult). bad analysis, shows use fishyenergy package get bioenergetics parameters species, find water temperature data, compute weight gain species site – use better data!","code":"library(fishyenergy) closest_species <- fb4_closest_species(\"Thymallus arcticus\") #> Progress [---------------------------------] 0/468 (  0%) ?sProgress [==============================] 468/468 (100%)  0s                                                            [1] \"Sources for Thymallus arcticus baicalensis are: Hartman and Jensen 2016\" closest_species #> [1] \"Thymallus arcticus baicalensis\" print(fb4_data$Source[fb4_data$Sci_Name == closest_species]) #> [1] \"Hartman and Jensen 2016\" BEM <- bem_from_fb4(closest_species, CP=0.5, FA=0.1018428, UA=0.08743042, SDA=0.150819, ED=3983.646) stations <- water_stations_find(state=\"TN\") knitr::kable(head(stations[,1:6], 5), caption=\"First 5 stations in Tennessee with water temperature data\") little_river_row <- stations[stations$station_nm == \"LITTLE RIVER ABOVE TOWNSEND, TN\",] little_river_id <- paste0(\"USGS-\", little_river_row$site_no) raw_temps <- daily_temperature_raw(little_river_id, start_date=\"2022-11-01\", end_date=\"2024-02-01\") plot(raw_temps$ActivityStartDate, raw_temps$ResultMeasureValue, pch=19, xlab=\"Date\", ylab=\"Temperature (C)\", main=\"Little River Temperature Data\", bty=\"n\") interpolated_temps <- daily_temperature_interpolate(raw_temps, minimum_fraction_missing=0.05, start_date=\"2023-01-01\", end_date=\"2023-12-31\") plot(raw_temps$ActivityStartDate, raw_temps$ResultMeasureValue, pch=19, xlab=\"Date\", ylab=\"Temperature (C)\", main=\"Little River Temperature Data\", bty=\"n\") points(interpolated_temps$date, interpolated_temps$temp, col=\"red\", pch='.') station_result <- single_station_compute(T_vector=interpolated_temps$temp, BEM=BEM) plot(interpolated_temps$date, station_result$W2_cum, xlab=\"Date\", ylab=\"Mass (g)\", type='l', bty=\"n\", main=\"Weight Gain in Little River\") all_organisms <- loop_species_across_station(interpolated_temps$temp)  all_organisms$SpeciesStage <- all_organisms$common  for (i in 1:nrow(all_organisms)) {   if (nchar(all_organisms$life_stage[i]) > 0) {     all_organisms$common[i] <- paste0(all_organisms$SpeciesStage[i], \" (\", all_organisms$life_stage[i], \")\")   } }  somewhat_surviving_organisms <- all_organisms[!is.na(all_organisms$W2_cum),]     weird_taxa <- unique(c(subset(somewhat_surviving_organisms, ProportionStartingWeight < 0.5 | ProportionStartingWeight > 25)$SpeciesStage, all_organisms$SpeciesStage[is.na(all_organisms$W2_cum)]))   feasible_organisms <- somewhat_surviving_organisms[!somewhat_surviving_organisms$SpeciesStage %in% weird_taxa,]  library(ggplot2) library(ggrepel) library(dplyr)  last_points <- feasible_organisms |>   group_by(SpeciesStage) |>   slice_max(order_by = julian, n = 1)   ggplot(feasible_organisms, aes(x=julian, y=ProportionStartingWeight, color=SpeciesStage)) +   geom_line() + theme_minimal() +   geom_text_repel(     data = last_points,     aes(label = SpeciesStage),     hjust = 0, nudge_x = 5, size = 3, show.legend = FALSE, segment.color = 'gray', box.padding = 0.3, max.overlaps = Inf   )  +   labs(x=\"Day of year\", y=\"Relative mass\") + xlim(c(0, 500)) + theme(legend.position = \"none\") dead_organisms <- sort(unique(somewhat_surviving_organisms$SpeciesStage[which(somewhat_surviving_organisms$W2_cum<0)]))"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"in-this-vignette-you-will","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"In this vignette, you will:","title":"Getting Started","text":"Learn fundamentals bioenergetics modeling (BEM) visualizing mass dependent temperature-dependent physiological rates. Simulate daily energetics growth fish year. Explore effects temporal extrinsic parameters like temperature, proportion maximum consumption (CP), activity multiplier (ACT), prey energy density. Compare energetics growth among representative warmwater coldwater species.","code":""},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"section","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"First, install fishyenergy package load library. Dependencies include reshape2, stringr, terra, sf, lubridate, ggplot2. libraries automatically loaded.","code":"setwd(\"D:\\\\UTSA_v01\\\\UTSA_research_v01\\\\project_BEM_package_v01\\\\fishyenergy\\\\\") devtools::load_all() library(reshape2) library(stringr) library(terra) library(sf) library(lubridate) library(ggplot2)"},{"path":[]},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"temperature-dependent-physiological-rates","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"In this vignette, you will:","what":"1) Temperature-dependent physiological rates","title":"Getting Started","text":"Next, let’s look temperature-dependent physiological rates species. Start loading intrinsic bioenergetics parameters five example species. parameters published various authors since 1980s available Fish Bioenergetics 4.0 (FB4)","code":"options(width = 500) data(parms_fb4)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"section-2","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"Let’s retain one focal species, largemouth bass, look data. first column shows Latin name snake case. columns like lwA lwB intercept slope length-weight equation. Still columns like CA CB intercept slope mass dependent consumption equation (negative power law). ’s also specific dynamic action (.e., proportion energy consumed allocated cost digestion), predator energy density, many variables defined Hanson et al. (1997).","code":"options(width = 500) parms.micsal <- parms_fb4[parms_fb4$genus_species == \"micropterus_salmoides\",] parms.micsal #>           genus_species LifeStage           Source    lwA   lwB CEQ   CA     CB   CQ  CTO CTM CTL CK1 CK4 REQ       RA     RB     RQ    RTO RTM RTL RK1 RK4     SDA    FA      UA pred_ED #> 2 micropterus_salmoides     adult Rice et al. 1983 0.0226 2.781   2 0.33 -0.325 2.65 27.5  37  NA  NA  NA   1 0.008352 -0.355 0.0313 0.0196   0   0   1   0 0.15848 0.104 0.08817    4184"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"section-3","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"Let’s visualize effect temperature consumption, respiration, various physiological rates largemouth bass. , use bem_curve function needs supplied essential arguments. Set mass reasonable amount (454 grams ~ 1 pound). plot curve ecologically realistic temperature range 5°C 35°C. specify intrinsic parameters largemouth bass corresponding consumption respiration functions… 2 1, respectively.  Notice consumption curve increases exponentially optimum temperature ~28°C declines higher temperatures. curve shape characteristic consumption function 2. contrast, see respiration curve increases exponentially full range temperatures (.e., optimum beyond respiration declines). curve shape characteristic respiration function 1. Compare curves plotted activity multiplier changed 1.0 2.0, thus simulating predator fish 2 times metabolic demand previous predator fish rest. Notice red respiration curve higher consequently black surplus curve lower.","code":"bem_curve(M = 454,           T = 5:35,           CP = 1.0,           ACT = 1.0,           prey_ED = 4000,           parms.intrinsic = parms.micsal,           C_eq = 2,           R_eq = 1) bem_curve(M = 454,           T = 5:35,           CP = 1.0,           ACT = 2.0,           prey_ED = 4000,           parms.intrinsic = parms.micsal,           C_eq = 2,           R_eq = 1)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"section-4","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"clear largemouth bass perform well higher temperatures know members warmwater thermal guild. Let’s visualize effect temperature physiological rates putative coldwater species, brook trout. Notice consumption respiration equations brook trout 2 2, respectively.","code":"bem_curve(M = 454,           T = 5:35,           CP = 1.0,           ACT = 1.0,           prey_ED = 4000,           parms.intrinsic = parms_fb4[parms_fb4$genus_species == \"salvelinus_fontinalis\",],           C_eq = 2,           R_eq = 2)"},{"path":[]},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"grow-a-fish","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"In this vignette, you will:","what":"2) Grow a fish!","title":"Getting Started","text":"First, simulate (.e., create fake) temperature time series mean daily water temperature every day calendar year. using sine wave function, setting mean annual temperature annual amplitude values realistic temperate waterbody ice winter. Plot time series mean daily water temperature.","code":"parm.tmean <- 16        # mean annual temperature parm.ampli <- 13        # temperature amplitude julian <- seq(1,365,1) WT_mean <- parm.ampli * sin((2*(pi/365)) * julian - 2) + parm.tmean temps_fake <- data.frame(julian, WT_mean) ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() +   labs(x = \"Day of year\", y = \"Water temperature (°C)\") +   scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,366), breaks = seq(0, 360, 60)) +    scale_y_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,40), breaks = seq(0, 40, 10)) +    geom_line(aes(x = julian, y = WT_mean), temps_fake, linewidth = 1)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"section-6","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"Next, use bem_grow function simulate daily bioenergetic budget largemouth bass. need load parameters potentially vary temporally. dataframe 365 rows representing day year. five noteworthy columns (.e., variables): CP proportion maximum consumption decrease prey quantity declines exploitative competition increases. ACT activity multiplier value zero indicate metabolic costs beyond rest. Maintaining position flowing stream, avoiding predators, pursuing prey, engaging reproductive behavior increase ACT. next two parameters (gsi_f gsi_m) allow user model somatic growth reproductively mature fish allocates energy gonadal tissue. Separate parameters provided females males sexes invest different amounts energy gonads–gsi_f gsi_m, respectively. default data assume reproductively immature fish (.e., gsi = 0). Modifying prey energy density (prey_ED) year can simulate ontogenetic diet shifts changes prey quality. numerous published estimates prey energy density common prey items like invertebrates, fish, .","code":"options(width = 500) data(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) dim(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) #> [1] 365   6 head(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) #>       date CP ACT gsi_f gsi_m prey_ED #> 1 1/1/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 2 1/2/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 3 1/3/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 4 1/4/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 5 1/5/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 6 1/6/2025  1   1     0     0    3698"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"section-7","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"Next, use bem_grow function simulate daily bioenergetic budget largemouth bass. Start fish 454 grams ~1 pound. need load parameters potentially vary temporally.","code":"grow.micsal <- bem_grow(start_M2 = 454,                         temperature = temps_fake,                         parms.intrinsic = parms.micsal,                         parms.temporal = parms_temporal_DEFAULT,                         C_eq = 2,                         R_eq = 1)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Getting_Started.html","id":"section-8","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"output bem_grow quite extensive. 365 rows representing day year 43(!) variables. Let’s look variables: first eight variables represent inputs ’re already familiar like temperature, CP, ACT, gsi, . remaining variables outputs including consumption (C), respiration (R), egestion (F), excretion (U), specific dynamic action (SDA), somatic mass (MS), gonadal mass females males (MG). “1” version variables units energy (joules) “2” version variables units mass (grams). “_ins” version variables represent