revious_headings":"","what":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"better visualize summer growth, plot m2_ins. horizontal red line y = 0, meaning predator fish energy deficit line (winter) energy surplus line (summer).  Let’s manually adjust CP ACT see affects growth. dig literature, CP values often around 0.6 ACT values often around 1.4. Change values parms_temporal_DEFAULT dataframe, re-run bem_grow function, re-plot growth. seems reasonable 454 gram fish grow 548 grams year–, 1.0 pounds 1.2 pounds.  Finally, let’s look mass, length, condition change time. Bioenergetics models biologically realistic simulate daily energy balance–, either deficits surpluses. Mass can decrease increase time whereas length can increase energy surpluses realistically decrease energy deficits. bem_grow function uses axiom simulate changes Fulton condition factor time demonstrated : previous plot shows mass predator fish starts 454 grams January 1st decreases 120 (~April). predator fish grows mass length day 290 (~October). Plot length (cm) time see length change periods energy deficit increases energy surplus.  mass decreases length stays , Fulton condition decrease.","code":"ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() +   labs(x = \"Day of year\", y = \"Daily mass change (g)\") +   scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,366), breaks = seq(0, 360, 60)) +   geom_hline(yintercept = 0, color = \"red\") +   geom_line(aes(x = julian, y = M2_ins), grow.micsal, linewidth = 1) parms_temporal_DEFAULT$CP <- 0.6 parms_temporal_DEFAULT$ACT <- 1.4  grow.micsal.real <- bem_grow(start_M2 = 454,                              temperature = temps_fake,                              parms.intrinsic = parms.micsal,                              parms.temporal = parms_temporal_DEFAULT,                              C_eq = 2,                              R_eq = 1) ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() +   labs(x = \"Day of year\", y = \"Accumulated mass (g)\") +   scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,366), breaks = seq(0, 360, 60)) +   geom_line(aes(x = julian, y = M2_cum), grow.micsal.real, linewidth = 1) ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() +   labs(x = \"Day of year\", y = \"Total length (cm)\") +   scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,366), breaks = seq(0, 360, 60)) +   geom_line(aes(x = julian, y = L_cum), grow.micsal.real, linewidth = 1) ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() +   labs(x = \"Day of year\", y = \"Fulton condition\") +   scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,366), breaks = seq(0, 360, 60)) +   geom_line(aes(x = julian, y = K), grow.micsal.real, linewidth = 1)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Mapping_BrookTrout.html","id":"in-this-vignette-you-will","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"In this vignette, you will:","title":"Mapping Brook trout","text":"Explore spatial variation performance Brook trout (Salvelinus fontinalis) interconfluence stream reaches Little River watershed, tributary upper Tennessee River system drains Blue Ridge Mountains Great Smoky Mountains National Park Tennessee, USA. Use bioenergetics modeling simulate individual performance metrics across 513 interconfluence stream reaches (lotic habitat patches). Create maps showing spatially-contiguous variation performance metrics drivers spatial variation (e.g., temperature).","code":""},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Mapping_BrookTrout.html","id":"section","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Mapping Brook trout","title":"Mapping Brook trout","text":"First, install fishyenergy package load library. Dependencies include reshape2, stringr, terra, sf, lubridate, ggplot2. libraries automatically loaded.","code":"setwd(\"D:\\\\UTSA_v01\\\\UTSA_research_v01\\\\project_BEM_package_v01\\\\fishyenergy\\\\\") devtools::load_all() library(reshape2) library(stringr) library(terra) library(sf) library(lubridate) library(ggplot2)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Mapping_BrookTrout.html","id":"section-1","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Mapping Brook trout","title":"Mapping Brook trout","text":"Next, load intrinsic bioenergetics parameters five example species. parameters published various authors since 1980s available Fish Bioenergetics 4.0 (FB4).","code":"data(parms_fb4)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Mapping_BrookTrout.html","id":"section-2","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Mapping Brook trout","title":"Mapping Brook trout","text":"Let’s retain one focal species, Brook trout, look data. first column shows Latin name snake case. columns like lwA lwB intercept slope length-weight equation. Still columns like CA CB intercept slope mass dependent consumption equation (negative power law). ’s also specific dynamic action (.e., proportion energy consumed allocated cost digestion), predator energy density, many variables defined Hanson et al. (1997).","code":"options(width = 500) parms.salfon <- parms_fb4[parms_fb4$genus_species == \"salvelinus_fontinalis\",] parms.salfon #>           genus_species      LifeStage                                                                         Source     lwA  lwB CEQ     CA      CB    CQ CTO CTM CTL CK1 CK4 REQ     RA     RB  RQ  RTO RTM RTL RK1 RK4   SDA    FA     UA pred_ED #> 5 salvelinus_fontinalis juvenile_adult Troia modified from: Elliot 1976; Hartman and Sweka 2003; Hartman and Cox 2008 0.00881 3.04   2 0.3013 -0.3055 7.274  17  21  NA  NA  NA   2 0.0132 -0.265 4.5 20.2  25  NA  NA  NA 0.172 0.212 0.0314    4162"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Mapping_BrookTrout.html","id":"section-3","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Mapping Brook trout","title":"Mapping Brook trout","text":"Next, need load parameters potentially vary temporally. dataframe 365 rows representing day year. five noteworthy columns (.e., variables): CP proportion maximum consumption decrease prey quantity declines exploitative competition increases. ACT activity multiplier value zero indicate metabolic costs beyond rest. Maintaining position flowing stream, avoiding predators, pursuing prey, engaging reproductive behavior increase ACT. next two parameters (gsi_f gsi_m) allow user model somatic growth reproductively mature fish allocates energy gonadal tissue. Separate parameters provided females males sexes invest different amounts energy gonads–gsi_f gsi_m, respectively. default data assume reproductively immature fish (.e., gsi = 0). Modifying prey energy density (prey_ED) year can simulate ontogenetic diet shifts changes prey quality. numerous published estimates prey energy density common prey items like invertebrates, fish, .","code":"options(width = 500) data(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) dim(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) #> [1] 365   6 head(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) #>       date CP ACT gsi_f gsi_m prey_ED #> 1 1/1/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 2 1/2/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 3 1/3/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 4 1/4/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 5 1/5/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 6 1/6/2025  1   1     0     0    3698"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Mapping_BrookTrout.html","id":"section-4","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Mapping Brook trout","title":"Mapping Brook trout","text":"Let’s modify CP ACT realistic values. Specifically, use CP = 0.50 ACT = 1.60 parameter values validated behind scenes using bem_validate function. See Validation vignette details.","code":"options(width = 500) parms_temporal_DEFAULT$CP = 0.50 parms_temporal_DEFAULT$ACT = 1.60 head(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) #>       date  CP ACT gsi_f gsi_m prey_ED #> 1 1/1/2025 0.5 1.6     0     0    3698 #> 2 1/2/2025 0.5 1.6     0     0    3698 #> 3 1/3/2025 0.5 1.6     0     0    3698 #> 4 1/4/2025 0.5 1.6     0     0    3698 #> 5 1/5/2025 0.5 1.6     0     0    3698 #> 6 1/6/2025 0.5 1.6     0     0    3698"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Mapping_BrookTrout.html","id":"section-5","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Mapping Brook trout","title":"Mapping Brook trout","text":"Let’s explore stream temperature data produced Herrera-Rodriguez, Giam, Troia, others part Southeastern Climate Adaptation Science Center (CASC) project. dataset provides daily mean temperatures 76,553 interconfluence stream segments Tennessee Cumberland River systems every year 1982 2022. segments represent National Stream Internet (NSI; modified National Hydrography Dataset) include geographic (e.g., elevation sea level) hydrographic (e.g., catchment area) covariates segments. fishyenergy, extracted daily temperatures 2022 covariates 513 interconfluence stream segments Little River watershed. Let’s load dataset called temps_casc fishyenergy. dataset 513 rows representing different interconfluence stream segment 366 columns representing unique segment ID (COMID) followed 365 julian days.","code":"options(width = 2000) data(temps_casc) dim(temps_casc) #> [1] 513 366 head(temps_casc[,1:91]) #>           COMID julian001 julian002 julian003 julian004 julian005 julian006 julian007 julian008 julian009 julian010 julian011 julian012 julian013 julian014 julian015 julian016 julian017 julian018 julian019 julian020 julian021 julian022 julian023 julian024 julian025 julian026 julian027 julian028 julian029 julian030 julian031 julian032 julian033 julian034 julian035 julian036 julian037 julian038 julian039 julian040 julian041 julian042 julian043 julian044 julian045 julian046 julian047 julian048 julian049 julian050 julian051 julian052 julian053 julian054 julian055 julian056 julian057 julian058 julian059 julian060 julian061 julian062 julian063 julian064 julian065 julian066 julian067 julian068 julian069 julian070 julian071 julian072 julian073 julian074 julian075 julian076 julian077 julian078 julian079 julian080 julian081 julian082 julian083 julian084 julian085 julian086 julian087 julian088 julian089 julian090 #> 1 COMID22130003      14.0      13.6  