"","what":"Mapping Brook trout","title":"Mapping Brook trout","text":"relationship environmental variables brook trout performance? Let’s plot see. point represents different interconfluence stream reach Little River watershed.","code":"ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() + theme(legend.position = \"none\") +     labs(x = \"Elevation (masl)\", y = \"End-of-year mass (g)\") +     geom_point(aes(x = ElevCat, y = M2_cum), vect.perf.casc, shape = 16, size = 3, alpha = 0.5) +     geom_smooth(aes(x = ElevCat, y = M2_cum), vect.perf.casc, method = \"gam\") ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() + theme(legend.position = \"none\") +     labs(x = \"Catchment area (square km)\", y = \"End-of-year mass (g)\") +     scale_x_log10() +     geom_point(aes(x = TotDASqKM, y = M2_cum), vect.perf.casc, shape = 16, size = 3, alpha = 0.5) +     geom_smooth(aes(x = TotDASqKM, y = M2_cum), vect.perf.casc, method = \"gam\")"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"in-this-vignette-you-will","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"In this vignette, you will:","title":"Validation","text":"Learn validate bionenergetics model. , simulate 365 days growth use empirical length--age estimates field study lab growth experiment identify consumption (CP) activity (ACT) values produce realistic growth. Visualize growth trajectories. Visualize validated combinations CP ACT.","code":""},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"First, install fishyenergy package load library. Dependencies include reshape2, stringr, terra, sf, lubridate, ggplot2. libraries automatically loaded.","code":"setwd(\"D:\\\\UTSA_v01\\\\UTSA_research_v01\\\\project_BEM_package_v01\\\\fishyenergy\\\\\") devtools::load_all() library(reshape2) library(stringr) library(terra) library(sf) library(lubridate) library(ggplot2)"},{"path":[]},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"temperature-dependent-physiological-rates","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"In this vignette, you will:","what":"Temperature-dependent physiological rates","title":"Validation","text":"Next, let’s look temperature-dependent physiological rates species. Start loading intrinsic bioenergetics parameters five example species. parameters published various authors since 1980s available Fish Bioenergetics 4.0 (FB4)","code":"options(width = 500) data(parms_fb4)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section-2","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"Let’s retain one focal species, largemouth bass, look data. first column shows Latin name snake case. columns like lwA lwB intercept slope length-weight equation. Still columns like CA CB intercept slope mass dependent consumption equation (negative power law). ’s also specific dynamic action (.e., proportion energy consumed allocated cost digestion), predator energy density, many variables defined Hanson et al. (1997).","code":"options(width = 500) parms.micsal <- parms_fb4[parms_fb4$genus_species == \"micropterus_salmoides\",] parms.micsal #>           genus_species LifeStage           Source    lwA   lwB CEQ   CA     CB   CQ  CTO CTM CTL CK1 CK4 REQ       RA     RB     RQ    RTO RTM RTL RK1 RK4     SDA    FA      UA pred_ED #> 2 micropterus_salmoides     adult Rice et al. 1983 0.0226 2.781   2 0.33 -0.325 2.65 27.5  37  NA  NA  NA   1 0.008352 -0.355 0.0313 0.0196   0   0   1   0 0.15848 0.104 0.08817    4184"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section-3","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"Next, need load parameters potentially vary temporally. dataframe 365 rows representing day year. five noteworthy columns (.e., variables): CP proportion maximum consumption decrease prey quantity declines exploitative competition increases. ACT activity multiplier value zero indicate metabolic costs beyond rest. Maintaining position flowing stream, avoiding predators, pursuing prey, engaging reproductive behavior increase ACT. next two parameters (gsi_f gsi_m) allow user model somatic growth reproductively mature fish allocates energy gonadal tissue. Separate parameters provided females males sexes invest different amounts energy gonads–gsi_f gsi_m, respectively. default data assume reproductively immature fish (.e., gsi = 0). Modifying prey energy density (prey_ED) year can simulate ontogenetic diet shifts changes prey quality. numerous published estimates prey energy density common prey items like invertebrates, fish, .","code":"options(width = 500) data(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) dim(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) #> [1] 365   6 head(parms_temporal_DEFAULT) #>       date CP ACT gsi_f gsi_m prey_ED #> 1 1/1/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 2 1/2/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 3 1/3/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 4 1/4/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 5 1/5/2025  1   1     0     0    3698 #> 6 1/6/2025  1   1     0     0    3698"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section-4","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"Let’s explore lake temperature data modified open-source LakeTEMP dataset (Korver et al. 2024). brief, dataset provides monthly lake surface temperatures 1,427,688 lakes throughout world well geographic (e.g., elevation sea level) bathymetric (e.g., lake depth) covariates lakes. fishyenergy, extracted monthly temperatures 8,046 lakes 15 southeastern US states plus Puerto Rico. used generalized additive model fitted monthly temperatures interpolate temperatures daily resolution needed bioenergetics modeling. Let’s load dataset called temps_lake fishyenergy. dataset 8,046 rows representing different lake 366 columns representing unique lake ID (Hylak_id) fo