0069\",2:366]) WT.TN <- as.numeric(temps_lake[temps_lake$Hylak_id == \"Hylak_id.0000810\",2:366]) temps_lake.subset <- data.frame(date,                                 WT.FL,                                 WT.TN)  ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() +     labs(x = \"Day of year\", y = \"Water temperature (°C)\") +     scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,365), breaks = seq(0,360,60)) +     scale_y_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,40), breaks = seq(0,40,10)) +     geom_line(aes(x = date, y = WT.FL), temps_lake.subset, color = \"#005030\", linewidth = 1) +     geom_line(aes(x = date, y = WT.TN), temps_lake.subset, color = \"#FF8200\", linewidth = 1)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section-7","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"data wrangling get Watts Bar temperature format needed simulate fish growth. , dataframe 365 rows representing day calendar year two columns representing date daily mean water temperature.","code":"options(width = 500) temps_WattsBar <- temps_lake[temps_lake$Hylak_id == \"Hylak_id.0000810\",2:366] temps_WattsBar <- data.frame(t(temps_WattsBar)) colnames(temps_WattsBar) <- c(\"WT_mean\") rownames(temps_WattsBar) <- NULL temps_WattsBar$date <- 1:365 temps_WattsBar <- temps_WattsBar[,c(\"date\",\"WT_mean\")]"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section-8","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"Next, use bem_grow function simulate daily bioenergetic budget age-1 largemouth bass Watts Bar. realistic largemouth bass living East Tennessee reservoir reaches 15 cm total length mass 42 grams end first year, let’s simulate growth fish.","code":"grow.micsal_WattsBar <- bem_grow(start_M2 = 57,                                  temperature = temps_WattsBar,                                  parms.intrinsic = parms.micsal,                                  parms.temporal = parms_temporal_DEFAULT,                                  C_eq = 2,                                  R_eq = 1)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section-9","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"Plot time series m2_cum. predator fish starts year 57 grams (2 ounces) ends year whopping 1831 grams (~4 pounds), totally unrealistic. assumed predator fish limitless food (CP = 1), sets shop one place never moves (ACT = 1), allocates energy reproduction (gsi_ = 0).","code":"ggplot(NULL) + theme_classic() +   labs(x = \"Day of year\", y = \"Mass (g)\") +   scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0,0), limits = c(0,366), breaks = seq(0, 360, 60)) +   geom_line(aes(x = date, y = M2_cum), grow.micsal_WattsBar, linewidth = 1)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section-10","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"Obviously CP=1 ACT=1 unrealistic, realistic values? Unclear! parameters difficult estimate wild fish, literature describing ways approaches developed bem_validate function address challenge using alterantive approach. function identify pairs CP ACT values produce realistic growth. Inputs bem_validate include subset ’re familiar bem_grow including temperature, parms.intrinsic, parms.temporal, C_eq, R_eq. Three new inputs include: Total length cm start year (start_L). use total length input instead mass lengths--age commonly presented literature masses--age. function just uses parms_fb4 convert inputted start_L mass running bem_grow function. example , use 15.0 cm TL reported typical largemouth bass start second year life (age-1) Tennessee reservoirs Etnier & Starnes 1993. expected total length cm end year based empirically-derived growth (end_L.empirical). example , use 26.8 cm TL reported typical largemouth bass end second year life (age-1) Tennessee reservoirs Etnier & Starnes 1993. number CP ACT parameter sets test growth validation. CP values drawn uniform probability distribution (base::runif) minimum 0.0 maximum 1.0. ACT values also drawn uniform distribution minimum maximum 1.0 4.0, respectively.","code":"vali.micsal_WattsBar <- bem_validate(start_L = 15.0,                                      end_L.empirical = 26.8,                                      temperature = temps_WattsBar,                                      parms.intrinsic = parms.micsal,                                      parms.temporal = parms_temporal_DEFAULT,                                      C_eq = 2,                                      R_eq = 1,                                      resamp.n = 1000)"},{"path":"https://bomeara.github.io/fishyenergy/articles/Validation.html","id":"section-11","dir":"Articles","previous_headings":"","what":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"Output bem_validate list length five: first list element run time. bem_validate function, like bem_project function, takes time run time increases number parameter sets resampled. first graphical output total length plotted function julian day. separate line plotted resampled parameter set finishes year. Gray lines represent parameter sets finish year (.e., fish die end year). Red lines represent parameter sets within +/- 5% empirically-derived end--year total length. latter shown horizontal dashed blue line.  graphical output shows resampled CP ACT parameter sets two-dimensional space. Gray symbols represent parameter sets finish year (.e., fish die end year). Red symbols represent parameter sets within +/- 5% empirical end--year total length. Low CP high ACT plot lower right represent low growth parameter sets. Alternatively, high CP low ACT plot upper left represent high growth parameter sets. Parameter sets low CP low ACT high CP high ACT represent intermediate growth typically correspond empirical growth (.e., represent realistic/validated parameter sets).  last two outputs tabular. first, year.end.perform, number rows equal resamp.n representing resampled parameter set. seven columns representing resampID 1 resamp.n, resampled parameters CP ACT, simulated end--year mass (M2_cum) total length (L_cum), empirically-derived end--year total length (end_L), Boolean variable indicating whether simulated end--year total length within +/-5% empirically-derived end--year total length. Note parameter sets yield full year growth simulation return NAs FALSEs. Let’s look CP ACT values 14 validated sets. can use one projecting/mapping. See three mapping vignettes. tabular output, daily.output, list number elements equal resamp.n representing resampled parameter set. element dataframe 365 rows 43 columns produced bem_grow function.","code":"vali.micsal_WattsBar$run.time #> [1] \"Run time is 6.3 minutes for 1000 parameter sets\" options(width = 500) vali.micsal_WattsBar$plot_growth options(width = 500) vali.micsal_WattsBar$plot_parms options(width = 500) head(vali.micsal_WattsBar$year.end.perform) #>   resampID   CP  ACT  M2_cum L_cum end_L accurate #> 1        1 0.27 2.59      NA    NA  26.8    FALSE #> 2        2 0.37 3.05      NA    NA  26.8    FALSE #> 3        3 0.57 2.15      NA    NA  26.8    FALSE #> 4        4 0.91 3.86      NA    NA  26.8    FALSE #> 5        5 0.20 1.36      NA    NA  26.8    FALSE #> 6        6 0.90 1.12 1036.99  47.7  26.8    FALSE options(width = 500) vali.micsal_WattsBar$year.end.perform[vali.micsal_WattsBar$year.end.perform$accurate,c(\"CP\",\"ACT\")] #>       CP  ACT #> 41  0.82 1.69 #> 94  0.88 1.90 #> 269 0.62 1.17 #> 331 0.99 2.12 #> 369 0.70 1.43 #> 387 0.80 1.67 #> 570 0.96 2.08 #> 648 0.86 1.78 #> 660 0.87 1.81 #> 704 0.68 1.37 #> 759 0.96 2.07 #> 879 0.74 1.53 #> 950 0.82 1.68 #> 997 0.64 1.21 options(width = 500) head(vali.micsal_WattsBar$daily.output[[1]]) #>   date WT_mean pred_ED gsi_m gsi_f  CP ACT prey_ED C1_ins  C2_ins R1_ins   R2_ins F1_ins F2_ins U1_ins U2_ins SDA1_ins SDA2_ins   MS1_ins MS2_ins MG1_ins MG2